.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH RANMA-SEND 1 "" "ranma 0.1.0" "ranma manual"
.SH NAME
ranma\-send \- Type into a pane as if at its keyboard: \fBranma send \-p 3 \-e \*(Aqmake test\*(Aq\fR
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBranma send\fR [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-pane\fR] [\fB\-\-keys\fR] [\fB\-\-paste\fR] [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-\-enter\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIINPUT\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Type into a pane as if at its keyboard: \fBranma send \-p 3 \-e \*(Aqmake test\*(Aq\fR
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-pane\fR \fI<PANE>\fR
The pane (from \fBranma panes\fR); the one this runs in when left out
.TP
\fB\-\-keys\fR
Each argument is a key as a bind spells it: \fBranma send \-\-keys ctrl+c\fR
.TP
\fB\-\-paste\fR
Send the text as a paste, bracketed if the program asked for that
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-enter\fR
Press Enter after the text, as a key of its own a moment later, so a program that takes a fast burst for a paste still submits the line
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIINPUT\fR>
The text (arguments joined by spaces), or the keys
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBranma\fR(1)
